Rapid parallel detection of multi-targets has always been an exploration aim in electrochemiluminescence (ECL) assays. Herein, a multifunctional nanocomposite of Zr metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) $\boldsymbol{@}\mathrm{PEI}\boldsymbol{@}\mathrm{AuAg}$ nano­ clusters (NCs) with intense and stable dual-wavelength ECL was synthesized for the first time, and used to construct a new ECL biosensor for rapid simultaneous detection of dual targets. Notably, the novel ECL emitter Zr MOFs with high-performance was not only integrated with a co-reactant polyethyleneimine (PEI) to form a unique intramolecular self-enhancing structure, but also loaded a large number of another ECL emitter AuAg NCs, furthermore, AuAg NCs with superior electron transfer property can much enhance the electrical conduc­ tivity of the composites, thus achieving the goal of “killing three birds with one stone”. Moreover, a unique stable and rigid three-dimensional DNA tetrahedron (TDN) structure was connected with two quenching probes BHQ1 and BHQ3 and immobilized on the composites-modified electrode, so ECL emission of the nanocomposites at two wavelengths of $535\mathrm{nm}$ and $644~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were both quenched by resonance energy transfer (RET). In the presence of target miRNAs, the efficient DNA cycling double-amplification processes were performed by using exonuclease (T7 Exo) combined with DNA Walker, thus both quenching groups were separated to restore the ECL at two wavelengths, achieving simultaneous and rapid ECL detection of two miRNAs. Therefore, this present work not only opens a unique nanocomplex with dual wavelength ECL and self-enhancing performance, but also develops a highly sensitive ECL biosensor with promising value for rapid multi-target analysis in clinical fields.
## 1. Introduction
Electrochemiluminescence (ECL) is new detection method after chemiluminescence (CL) and photoluminescence (PL) in the optical fields, and it was caused by electron transfer between electroactive substances on the electrode surface (aW. Zhao et al., 2021). By com­ parison, CL has high background and short luminescence process, and PL shows strong optical background and is prone to photobleaching, while ECL does not require any external light source excitation, showing higher analytical sensitivity and accuracy (Chen et al., 2018a; Liu et al., 2021). Therefore ECL is considered a robust and reliable detection method for trace substances in modern analytical chemistry (Guo et al., 2020; Ma et al., 2021; Wong et al., 2020). At present, the ECL research field for multiple detection urgently needs to be expanded, while the traditional quantum dots (Yang et al., 2022), metal nanoclusters (Cao et al., 2022), metals, and covalent organic frameworks (Zhu et al., 2021; Li et al., 2021) with only a single luminescent property are far from meeting the needs of ECL development in this direction, thus the research and development of new composite materials with high per­ formance, low interference, multi-potential and multi-wavelength emission is particularly important. For example, Zhu et al. firstly re­ ported a dual-wavelength luminescent Janus with Ru and luminol for dual-targets detection (Han et al., 2022). Yuan et al. synthesized CdTe QDs modulated by black phosphorus nanosheets with both cathodic and anodic luminescence, which is expected to be used for multi-target detection (bJ. Zhao et al., 2021). Moreover, new materials with photo­ catalytic and electrocatalytic activities emerge continuously (Mahdi et al., 2022; Yousefi et al., 2021a; Yousefi et al., 2021b; Yousefi et al., 2019a). The ingenious applications of them will promote rapid devel­ opment of high performance ECL (Yousefi et al., 2017, 2019b).
In recent years, organic molecules were used as ligands to synthesize MOFs with ECL properties, but the aggregation-caused quenching (ACQ) phenomenon inevitably occurs due to the π-π stacking effect between the aggregated luminescent centrosomes (Chen et al., 2018b; Huang et al., 2019). In order to overcome this problem, Tang et al. proposed the aggregation-induced emission (AIE) effect in 2021 (Zhang et al., 2021). Contrary to the ACQ effect, AIE molecules show higher ECL signals than monomers in the aggregated state, but almost no ECL emission occurs in dilute solutions in the free dispersed state. Based on this principle, Wei et al. used $z\mathrm{r}$ ions as the central node and selected 1, 1, 2, 2-tetra (4-carboxylbiphenyl) ethylene $\mathrm{(H_{4}T C B P E)}$ molecules with AIE effect as linking ligands to prepare metal-organic frameworks $\mathrm{Zr}$ -TCBPE-MOF, referred to as Zr MOFs) with aggregation-induced electro­ chemiluminescence (AI-ECL) effect (Wei et al., 2014). After testing, it was found that neither $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TCBPE}$ monomer nor its aggregates could generate an ECL signal comparable to that of Zr MOFs, this may be due to the fact that $\mathrm{H_{4}T C B P E}$ as the active ligand optimizes the energy gap of the MOFs, resulting in a greatly improved luminescence quantum yield. In view of this, the predecessors named this fascinating phenomenon as the coordination-triggered ECL (CT-ECL) enhancement effect. However, when organic ligands are introduced, poor conductivity and low elec­ tron transfer efficiency are also accompanied, thus it is urgent to solve this problem (Huang et al., 2020).
As an emerging noble metal nanomaterial in recent years, gold-silver nanoclusters have shown great brilliance in the fields of optics and electricity. Due to its ultra-high biocompatibility, satisfactory electron transport efficiency, and excellent ECL performance, it exhibits incom­ parable advantages in the fields of biosensing, in vivo imaging, and catalytic activation (An et al., 2020; Huang et al., 2018; Kamalakannan et al., 2022). However, its instability and easy dispersion in electrode surface modification also limit its further application (Zhai et al., 2017). Therefore, it is important to construct a stable carrier by physical adsorption or chemical connection. For example, Zhuo et al. selected the DNA tetrahedral block (TDB) structure with many binding sites, strong structural rigidity and easy functionalization to load AuAg NCs for enhancing signal stability (Yang et al., 2020). Xiao and his colleagues synthesized a UIO-66 MOFs with a hierarchical pore-shell structure for grafting small molecules Ru, which can greatly enhance the ECL signal and stability of Ru by accelerating electron transfer (Huang et al., 2020). In addition to enhancing the ECL performance of material by optimizing the conductivity and stability, it is also an effective method to flexibly shorten the distance between the ECL emitter and co-reactant. For example, Wang et al. proposed a bimolecular electron transfer strategy by combining triethylamine with polymer dots, which greatly improved the ECL signal of polymer dots by shortening the electron transfer path (Wang et al., 2021a).
Inspired by the above ideas, PEI molecules with a large number of tertiary amine groups were used as co-reactants to anchor on Zr MOFs to achieve ultra-close electron exchange. Thus ECL of Zr MOFs was greatly improved while reducing energy loss. Subsequently, AuAg NCs with excellent electrocatalytic activity and electrical conductivity were in­ tegrated on the surface of $\operatorname{Zr}\operatorname{MOFs}(\varnothing\operatorname{PEI}$ to accelerate electron transfer rate, solving the electron flow problem of the carrier Zr MOFs. Mean­ while, Zr MOFs can in turn enhance the stability of AuAg NCs ECL. It is worth noting that the composite has both ECL properties of Zr MOFs at $535\mathrm{nm}$ and AuAg NCs at $644\mathrm{nm}$ wavelengths without interfering with each other. Compared with the traditional analytical methods that rely on spatial resolution strategies for multi-target detection (Wang et al., 2021b), the dual-wavelength biosensor using Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs composite can easily achieve rapid and simultaneous detection of mul­ tiple targets without any spatial division.
MicroRNA (miRNA) is involved in all aspects of immune response in human body, and its overexpression is closely related to diseases such as cancer, and inflammatory bowel disease, and even cardiovascular and cerebrovascular diseases (Bartel, 2018). For example, the over­ expression of miRNA-141 and miRNA-155 has been confirmed to be an important indicator of the pathogenesis of preeclampsia. Therefore, accurate detection of trace amounts of miRNAs is crucial for human health (Gebert and MacRae, 2019; Martins et al., 2022). However, due to the inherent facile degradation, homologous similarity, and ultra-low abundance of miRNAs, the simultaneous multiplex accurate detection of miRNAs is extremely difficult (Liu et al., 2019; Ye et al., 2019). Recently, ECL biosensors for miRNA detection have emerged frequently, but the dual-wavelength ECL sensors for simultaneous detection of multiple miRNAs under a single anode or cathode condition have not been re­ ported (Yang et al., 2022; Liu et al., 2022; Tian et al., 2021).
In order to realize the rapid detection of multi-target miRNAs at dual wavelengths, it is necessary to find suitable methods to improve the detection sensitivity of low-level target miRNAs. So far, the DNA walker amplification is regarded as an effective strategy to achieve target amplification, but traditional DNA walkers usually have the defects of limited walking area, slow reaction rate, and complicated strand displacement reaction (Zhang et al., 2018, 2020). However, a single-enzyme-assisted double-cycle DNA walker amplification strategy has come into people’s field of vision (Wang et al., 2018). As a well-designed double amplification strategy, it can not only greatly improve the conversion rate of target, but also effectively avoid the interference of external factors and reduce the sensitivity to environ­ ment (Xia et al., 2020).
In view of the above inspiration, Zr MOFs, PEI, and AuAg NCs can be integrated to form a trinity to show many advantages. Thus a unique multifunctional nanocomposite with dual-wavelength ECL was synthe­ sized, using Zr MOFs and AuAg NCs as double ECL emitters, PEI as the close co-reactant of Zr MOFs, and AuAg NCs as the electron transport sensitizer to ECL. Based on the perfect coincidence of the ECL optimal emission wavelengths of Zr MOFs and AuAg NCs with the ultraviolet maximum absorption wavelengths of two dark quenching groups BHQ1 and BHQ3, a dual-wavelength ECL biosensor based on the resonance energy transfer effect was constructed for simultaneous detection of dual targets. It is worth emphasizing that a new TDN structure with excellent mechanical rigidity and high structural stability was introduced to the biosensor, which not only effectively avoid the entanglement of probe DNA on electrode, and but also can connect different quenching groups to realize the simultaneous detection of multiple targets. Moreover, a high-efficiency DNA walker cyclic splicing strategy was used, thus the self-driven walking of the assembled DNA swing arm was triggered in the presence of T7-Exo and target, realizing double cascade detection of trace target miRNAs. The ECL signal values at $535\mathrm{nm}$ and $644\mathrm{nm}$ have good linear relationship with the amounts of miRNA-141 and miRNA155, which provides strong proof for the feasibility of the dualwavelength ECL biosensor for simultaneous detection of multiple tar­ gets, and more importantly, this provides a general detection method for the detection of other disease markers.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of Zr-TCBPE-MOF
The synthesis method of $z\mathbf{r}$ MOFs using $\mathrm{H_{4}T C B P E}$ molecules as li­ gands was slightly modified on the basis of the literature (Scheme 1A) (Wei et al., 2014). First, $30~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{H_{4}T C B P E}$ and $60~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{zrCl}_{4}$ were dissolved in $3.6~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF, followed by adding $0.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ , and mixing uniformly under ultrasonication. Next, the above-mixed solution was added to a stainless steel hydrothermal reactor lined with PTFE and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the reaction was completed and cooled, the product was washed three times with secondary water and absolute ethanol in turn. Finally, the bright yellow product collected by centrifugation was dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and stored for later use.
## 2.2. Synthesis of the gold-silver bimetallic nanoclusters
The functionalized gold-silver bimetallic nanoclusters were synthesized with reference to previous methods (Luo et al., 2012). Briefly, $13.05~\mathrm{mL}$ of secondary water, $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{\HAuCl}_{4}$ $(20~\mathrm{mM})$ , and $0.3\mathrm{mL}$ of ${\bf A g N O_{3}}$ $\mathrm{\Omega}^{20\mathrm{mM}}$ were added into a $50\mathrm{mL}$ three-necked flask in turn. After that, $0.45~\mathrm{mL}$ of GSH ( $\mathrm{100~mM)}$ solution was added to the above-mixed solution and stirred at room temperature for $10\mathrm{min}$ . After the mixture was evenly mixed, it was placed in a water bath at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and heated to reflux for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The synthesized AuAg NCs were centrifuged and purified by an ultrafiltration centrifuge tube (MWCO 3K) for $15\mathrm{min}$ , and then stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the dark.
## 2.3. Synthesis of Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites
The assembly process of Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites was systematically introduced in Scheme 1A. Specifically, the assembly process is divided into two steps. In the first step, $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of the Zr MOFs prepared before were dissolved in $\scriptstyle5{\mathrm{~mL}}$ of secondary water by sonicat­ ion, then $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of EDC $\mathrm{100~mM},$ ) and $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of NHS $(25~\mathrm{mM})$ solution were added and activated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $40~\mathrm{min}$ . After activation, it was ultrasonically mixed with ${}5\mathrm{mL}$ of PEI $(1\%)$ solution and incubated for 6 h, after which it was purified by centrifugation, and the product was redispersed in $5\mathrm{mL}$ of secondary water to prepare Zr MOFs@PEI com­ plexes. In the second step, $500\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of AuAg NCs purified by ultrafiltration and centrifugation were diluted in an equal volume of secondary water, and then $50\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of EDC and NHS solutions with the same concentration as before were added to activate them. Finally, $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of the previously synthesized $\mathrm{Zr~MOFs@PEI}$ complex was mixed with it and incubated under the same conditions as before, followed by purification and redispersion in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of secondary water to obtain the final product $z\mathrm{r}$ MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites.
## 2.4. Preparation of the three-dimensional DNA tetrahedron (TDN)
TDNs are assembled by a classical annealing procedure $\mathrm{\DeltaYu}$ et al., 2021). Specifically, $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of N4 (or N5, W1, W2) capture probe $(10\upmu\mathrm{M})$ in TE buffer $10~\mathrm{mM}$ Tris-HCl, $1\mathrm{mM}$ EDTA, $\mathrm{pH}8.0\dot{.}$ ) was mixed with the same volume and concentration of amino-modified DNA strands (N1, N2, and N3), then the mixed solution was heated in a water bath at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 min and then quickly transferred to a $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ environment for cooling to obtain four different stable TDN structures (TDN1\~TDN4).
## 2.5. Construction of the ECL biosensor
First, ITO electrode was sonicated in acetone, $1\mathrm{~M~NaOH}$ (mixed solution with a volume ratio of ethanol and secondary water of 1:1), and secondary water for $15\mathrm{min}$ in turn, so that the surface of the electrode was modified with hydroxyl groups. After drying, the ITO electrode was wrapped with a sealing film to obtain a square area of $0.25\:\mathrm{cm}^{2}\:(0.5\:\mathrm{cm}$ $\times0.5\mathrm{cm}\rangle$ .
$10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}~\mathsf{S}\mathsf{1}$ chain with quencher BHQ1 and S2 chain with quencher BHQ3 were hybridized with $40~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of TDN1 and TDN2 previ­ ously synthesized for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , to obtain two hybrids of TDN1 $^+$ S1-BHQ1 and TDN2 $+s2$ -BHQ3. Similarly, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ Blocker1 chain and Blocker2 chain were hybridized with $40\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of TDN3-Walker1 and TDN4-Walker2 synthesized previously for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively, to obtain two hybrids of TDN3-Walker1 $+$ Blocker1 and TDN4-Walker2 $+$ Blocker2.
Next, $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the previously prepared Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs solution spread evenly on the square area of the ITO electrode. After the film formation was stable, the surface was activated with a mixed so­ lution of $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ EDC $(100~\mathrm{mM})$ and NHS $(25~\mathrm{mM})$ (volume ratio 1:1). Then, $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of the mixed solution of the four TDN hybrids in equal proportions was drop-coated on the activated film for amide binding.
Finally, $1\mathrm{mM}$ mercaptoethanol (MCH) was used to seal the plate for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to complete the construction of the biosensor. All the electrode surface should be carefully cleaned with secondary water after each modifica­ tion step.
When the target miRNA-141 and 155 were analyzed, $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of different concentrations of target miRNAs were mixed with 5U of T7 Exo and incubated on the biosensor surface for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The concentration of the target analyte can be determined by detecting the ECL signal of the sensor.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Dual-wavelength biosensor mechanism for simultaneous detection of multiple microRNAs
The design principle of the dual-wavelength ECL biosensor based on a novel nanocomposite of Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs for simultaneous detection of miRNA-141 and miRNA-155 is demonstrated in Scheme 1. Firstly, the multifunctional Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites with dual-wavelength ECL emission properties were immobilized on the electrode, displaying high and stable ECL signals at two wavelengths of 535 and $644\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Importantly, PEI molecules as coreactants on Zr MOFs can achieve ultra-close electron exchange be­ tween them and significantly contributes to ECL emission intensity.
Moreover, a highly stable TDN rigid structure was formed by the assembly of four single-stranded DNAs that could specifically bind to each other, then different TDN structures with dark quencher (BHQ1 and BHQ3) and walkers were immobilized on the surface of Zr MOF­ $s@\operatorname{PEI}@\operatorname{AuAg}$ NCs via amide binding. Based on the resonance energy transfer effect, the ECL emission of the composites at $535\mathrm{nm}$ (Zr MOFs) was quenched by the dark quencher BHQ1, and the ECL emission at 644 nm (AuAg NCs) was quenched by BHQ3. In particular, the TDN rigid structure effectively solved the problem of surface DNA probe entan­ glement, and reduced steric hindrance. Next, in the presence of micro­ RNAs and T7 Exo, the targets were cycled for competitive cleavage of
Blockers, followed by cyclic separation of the quenching groups on TDN structure and cyclic cleavage by efficient DNA walker, thereby realizing recovery of ECL signal by releasing quenching groups. The increased ECL signals at two wavelengths showed good linear relationships with amounts of two targets. Based on this principle, the rapid simultaneous detection of multiple microRNAs was realized.
## 3.2. Characterization of Zr-TCBPE-MOF, AuAg NCs and Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites
Fig. 1A is the scanning electron microscope (SEM) image of ZrTCBPE-MOFs. It can be found that the synthesized Zr MOFs are hexag­ onal dumbbell sheets with sharp edges and corners, and their average size is about $3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Fig. 1B is the fluorescence (FL) emission spectrum of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -TCBPE-MOF with an emission peak at $535\mathrm{nm}$ ( $\mathbf{Ex}=320\mathbf{nm}\cdot$ . Fig. 1C is the transmission electron microscope (TEM) image of the synthesized AuAg NCs, showing clear morphology and basically uniform size. Inset shows the particle size distribution of AuAg NCs with an average particle size of $2.26\pm0.35\mathrm{nm}$ . Fig. 1D is the fluorescence emission spectrum of AuAg NCs at the excitation wavelength of $385\mathrm{nm}$ , and the fluorescence emission peak is $595\mathrm{nm}$ .
Fig. 2A is the SEM image of the Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nano­ composite. Compared with Zr MOFs, the edge of the nanocomposite has become smoother and the surface is slightly grainy. This should be caused by the coating of PEI molecules and the aggregation modification of AuAg NCs on the surface, which also indirectly proves the successful recombination of them. In addition, elemental mapping analysis on the Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites was performed. As expected, carbon, nitrogen, zirconium, gold, and silver elements were mainly detected, corresponding to the B–F maps in Fig. 2, respectively. Among them, carbon and zirconium elements come from Zr MOFs, nitrogen elements come from amino-rich PEI molecules, and gold and silver el­ ements come from AuAg NCs. In view of these results, the successful synthesis of Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites was demonstrated.
3.3. Gel electrophoresis analysis of 3D DNA tetrahedral assembly and cyclic shearing of DNA walker
The feasibility of the DNA walker cyclic shearing and the threedimensional DNA tetrahedral assembly process was verified by poly­ acrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE). The three-dimensional DNA tetrahedron assembly process is shown in Fig. 3A. Lane M represents Marker DNA, and lanes 1 to 4 show four types of single-stranded DNAs that can hybridize with each other, N1, N2, N3, and N4, respectively. When these four single-stranded DNAs hybridized to pairs or even three or four, new and higher bands were generated. For example, the new higher bands appearing in lanes 5 and 6 are the hybridization products of N1 with N2, and N2 with N3, respectively. The new higher bands appearing in lanes 7 and 8 are the hybridization products of three singlestranded DNAs of $N1+N2+N3$ and $N2\substack{+\mathrm{N}3+\mathrm{N}4}$ respectively. Finally, the new highest band that appears in lane 9 is the hybridization product of $\mathrm{N}1+\mathrm{N}2+\mathrm{N}3+\mathrm{N}4$ , indicating the three-dimensional DNA tetrahedron was formed.
The cyclic shearing process of DNA is shown in Fig. 3B. Lanes 1 to 4 are Target DNA, Blocker DNA, Walker DNA and N4 DNA, respectively. When Walker DNA hybridized with Blocker DNA, the hybrid doublestrand was generated, corresponding to the new band appearing in lane 5. Next, after adding Target DNA to the Walker $+$ Blocker hybrid duplex, a brand new band was obtained in lane 6, corresponding to the Target $+$ Blocker hybrid duplex. It is shown that Target DNA successfully replaced the Blocker DNA in the Walker $^+$ Blocker hybrid double-strand, thereby liberating the Walker DNA. Subsequently, the liberated Walker DNA combined with N4 DNA to generate a Walker $+{\mathsf{N}}4$ hybrid duplex, corresponding to the new band appearing in lane 8. Finally, under the specific recognition of T7 Exo, the Walker  N4 hybrid duplex and the Target $^+$ Blocker hybrid duplex were both cut, the corresponding duplex bands disappeared (lanes 7 and 9), while the lower new bands appeared (lane 9). Thus the DNA cyclic shearing system was successfully performed.
3.4. Feasibility of the ECL biosensing platform for simultaneous detection of multiple target miRNAs
A series of comparative experiments verified the feasibility of the ECL biosensing platform for simultaneous detection of multiple target miRNAs. Fig. 4A showed the UV–Vis absorption spectra of PEI mole­ cules, AuAg NCs, Zr MOFs, and their complex $\mathrm{Zr~MOFs@PEI@AuAg}$ NCs. It can be seen that the synthesized Zr MOFs showed broad UV absorption in the wavelength range of $280{-}450\ \mathrm{~nm}$ , and had the strongest UV absorption peaks at $325\mathrm{nm}$ and $415\mathrm{nm}$ . Correspondingly, AuAg NCs only showed a less obvious characteristic peak at ${395}\mathrm{nm}$ . It is worth noting that compared with Zr MOFs, $\mathrm{Zr~MOFs@PEI@AuAg~NCs}$ composites and AuAg NCs showed more similar UV characteristic peaks, which may be caused by the PEI coating on the surface of Zr MOFs.
The dual enhancement of AuAg NCs and PEI molecules to Zr MOFs ECL was studied. In Fig. 4B, the Zr MOFs without modifying any ma­ terial showed a certain ECL signal at $535\mathrm{nm}$ in the buffer solution with TEA co-reactant. However, due to the limitation of the distance between TEA and Zr MOFs, the ECL signal value was low (curve a). After the complexation of PEI molecules, Zr MOFs showed enhanced ECL signal due to the effect of intramolecular coreaction-like acceleration effect (curve b). Correspondingly, after the modification of AuAg NCs with excellent electron transport efficiency, the conductivity of Zr MOFs was enhanced and the ECL signal was further elevated (curve c), which further proved that Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs displayed excellent ECL performance.
Fig. S1 showed the ECL responses of bare electrode (a), AuAg NCs (b), Zr MOFs@PEI (c), and Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs (d) in PBS con­ taining $0.05{\mathrm{~M~}}$ TEA, it can be observed that Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs showed highest ECL signal at $1.5\mathrm{V}$ .
Fig. 4C and D are the ECL emission spectra of Zr MOFs, AuAg NCs, and the UV absorption spectra of dark quenching groups BHQ1, and BHQ3, respectively. It can be seen that the ECL emission peak of $z\mathrm{r}$ MOFs was at $535\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which corresponded to the UV absorption maximum wavelength of BHQ1. Similarly, the ECL optimal emission wavelength of AuAg NCs was at $644\mathrm{nm}$ , which also corresponded to the maximum UV absorption wavelength of BHQ3. Due to the difference of chromophore in the structures of BHQ1 and BHQ3, their UV absorption ranges and peak values are obviously different. The perfect overlap of the ECL emission and UV absorption peaks was not only a valid evidence for resonance energy transfer effect but also a strong guarantee for successful construction of dual-wavelength ECL biosensor. Therefore, it is feasible to use this dual-wavelength ECL sensing platform to simul­ taneously detect multiple miRNAs.
Fig. 4E and F are the ECL signals at $535\mathrm{nm}$ and $644~\mathrm{{nm}}$ before and after adding target miRNA-141 and miRNA-155, respectively. Compared with background responses (curves a), Zr MOFs and AuAg NCs showed very high ECL signals at $535\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4E, curve b) and 644 nm (Fig. 4F, curve b), respectively. After quenching with BHQ1 or BHQ3, both ECL signals obviously became very low (curves c), sug­ gesting effective ECL quenching was achieved. With simultaneous addition of two target miRNAs, the ECL signals at both wavelengths were recovered (curves d). This is due to the presence of target miRNAs that drive the DNA cyclic cleavage process, resulting in the removal of dark quenching groups BHQ1 and BHQ3 on the TDN structures, thus the ECL resonance energy transfer effect between the substrate nano­ composite and the dark quencher also disappeared. The results proved successful development of the ECL biosensor based on dual-wavelength ECL of Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs and resonance energy transfer for simultaneous detection of dual miRNAs.
The possible ECL emission mechanism of the dual-wavelength ECL system based on Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites is described as follows (Wei et al., 2014; Yang et al., 2020):
$$
\mathrm{Zr\mathrm{-}T C B P E^{+}\mathrm{-}P E I^{+}\rightarrow Z r\mathrm{-}T C B P E^{+}\mathrm{-}P E I+H^{+}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Zr\mathrm{-}T C B P E^{+}\mathrm{-}P E I\rightarrow Z r\mathrm{-}T C B P E^{*}\mathrm{-}P E I}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Zr\mathrm{-TCBPE^{\ast}\mathrm{-PEI}\rightarrow\mathrm{Zr\mathrm{-}T C B P E\mathrm{-}P E I+h v\left(535\ n m\right)}}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{TEA}-\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{TEA}^{+}
$$
$$
\mathrm{\AuAg\NCs+TEA^{+}\rightarrow T E A+A u A g\ N C s^{+}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{TEA}^{*}\to\mathrm{TEA}^{*}+\mathrm{H}^{+}
$$
$$
\mathrm{TEA^{*}+A u A g N C s}\rightarrow\mathrm{Et_{2}N^{+}C H C H_{3}+A u A g N C s^{-}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{AuAg\NCs^{+}+A u A g\ N C s^{-}\to A u A g\ N C s^{*}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{AuAg\NCs^{*}\rightarrow A u A g\ N C s+h v\ (644\ n m)}
$$
## 3.5. Optimization of experimental conditions
Different experimental conditions have impacts on ECL signals in the detection process. Thus, various experimental conditions were opti­ mized with $0.1~\mathrm{\mathrm{nM}}$ miRNAs concentration.
Firstly, the concentration of Zr MOFs as the carrier luminophore was optimized. As shown in Fig. S2A, with the increase of Zr MOFs concentration, the loading of Zr MOFs on the electrode increased, so the ECL signal at the emission wavelength of $535\mathrm{nm}$ increased and reached a maximum at $4{\mathrm{~mg~mL}}^{-1}$ . Afterward, the ECL signal was slightly reduced due to shedding of excess substances. Therefore, $4\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}\ Z r}$ MOFs were selected for the experiment.
Next, the amount of PEI as a co-reactant was optimized. Herein, PEI was coated on the surface of Zr MOFs. As the coating amount reached saturation, its enhancement effect on the Zr MOFs ECL also tended to be stable. It can be seen from Fig. S2B that the optimal amount of PEI is 1 mL.
When compounding AuAg NCs with $\mathrm{Zr~MOFs@PEI}$ , the amount of AuAg NCs had an important influence on the ECL signal of the nano­ composites at $644~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . As can be seen from Figure Fig. S2C, with the dilution of the AuAg NCs solution, the ECL signal at $644~\mathrm{{nm}}$ firstly remained unchanged and then decreased after double dilution. There­ fore, the AuAg NCs by double dilution were selected for sensing analysis.
The amount of dark quencher determines the quenching effect on the ECL signal of the nanocomposites. In Fig. S2D, with the increase of the concentration of dark quenchers BHQ1 and BHQ3, both ECL signals at $535\mathrm{nm}$ and $644~\mathrm{{nm}}$ decreased, and almost simultaneously reached the critical value at $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Therefore, $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ of BHQ1 and BHQ3 were chosen for the quenching of sense.
Finally, the dosage and digestion time of T7 Exo were optimized. A shown in Figs. S2E and F, with the increase of dosage and digestion time, the ECL signal values at two wavelengths recovered. When the amount of enzyme was ${}_{5\mathrm{~U~}}$ and the digestion time was $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the maximum value was reached. Thus 5 U and $120\mathrm{min}$ were the optimal amount and digestion time of T7 Exo.
## 3.6. EIS and CV characterizations of the biosensor preparation process
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and cyclic voltam­ metry (CV) were used to study assembly process of the biosensor. Fig. S3A shows the electron transfer resistance (Ret) of [Fe(CN)6]4-/3- on modified electrodes at different stages. Compared to bare ITO electrode, the Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites show a larger electronic impedance (curve b), which is due to their low electron transport rate. Next, with the loading of the negatively charged TDN structure, the impedance of the electrode further increased (curve c), because [Fe $(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]^{4-/3-}$ repelled negatively charged substances, which in turn affected the electron transport. Correspondingly, when the non-specific recognition sites on the modified electrode surface were capped with mercaptoethanol (MCH), MCH hindered the charge transfer between potassium ferricyanide and the electrode surface, resulting in a continued increase in electrode impedance (curve d). It should be noted that with the addition of miRNAs and T7 Exo, although the DNA on the electrode is reduced by shearing according to the principle, but the impedance of the modified electrode continued to increase (curve e), and the phenomenon was consistent with CV results. The reason may be that the physical residue of highly sterically hindered enzymes was present on the electrode surface.
In addition, the assembly process of the electrodes was also charac­ terized by cyclic voltammetry (CV). In Fig. S3B, the current intensity and reduction potential of the electrodes modified layer by layer with $z\mathrm{r}$ MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs, TDNs, MCH, miRNAs and T7 Exo gradually decreased, while the oxidation potential and the interval between po­ tential peaks gradually increased. This is because the existence of the above-mentioned substances will inhibit the transfer process of charge, and cause the amounts change of $\left[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}\right]^{3-/4-}$ at both oxidation state and reduction state on electrode. The CV and EIS have the correspond results, suggesting the construction of the biosensor.
## 3.7. Analytical performance of the dual-wavelength ECL biosensor
The analytical performance of the ECL-RET biosensor was studied. Fig. 5A is the detected ECL signals of the Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites at $535~\mathrm{nm}$ to different concentrations of miRNA-141. Fig. 5B is the ECL signals of the nanocomposite at $644~\mathrm{{nm}}$ to miRNA155 at different concentrations. It can be seen that with the increasing concentration of target miRNAs, the ECL signal values at both wave­ lengths showed a regular increase. Correspondingly, Fig. 5C showed the linear relationship between the ECL signal values and the concentration of target miRNAs. Among them, the linear equation of the ECL signal at $535~\mathrm{nm}$ is $\mathrm{I}_{535\mathrm{nm}}=36.48+2.06\mathrm{log}\mathrm{\bfC};$ and the linear calibration coef­ ficient $(\mathbb{R}^{2})$ is 0.997. Similarly, the corresponding linear equation at 644 nm is $\mathrm{I}_{644\mathrm{nm}}=25.26+1.49\mathrm{log}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ is 0.995. The results proved the well analytical performance of the dual-wavelength ECL biosensor for miR­ NAs assay.
Fig. 5D shows the stability of ECL signal values for 10 scan cycles at two wavelengths after adding target miRNAs, and the relative standard deviations (RSDs) were $1.4\%$ $\mathrm{535nm})$ and $1.2\%$ (644 nm), respectively. It can be seen that the dual-wavelength ECL biosensor shows well ac­ curacy and convincing stability in simultaneous detection of miRNAs. The detection limits for miRNAs are as low as 19 aM (miRNA-141) and $26\mathrm{aM}$ (miRNA-155), respectively. Compared with previously published methods for the detection of miRNAs (Table S2), this method has obvious detection advantages.
## 3.8. Selectivity and reproducibility of the ECL biosensor
The specificity of this ECL biosensor was verified by sensing several miRNAs with homologous similarity to the target miRNA-141 and miRNA-155. As can be seen from Fig. 5E, compared with the target miRNAs at the detection concentration of $0.1\mathrm{\nM}$ , the homologous similar miRNAs at the detection concentration of $10\mathrm{nM}$ did not show a recovery effect on the ECL signal. However, when a mixture containing both target miRNAs and cognate miRNAs as interferers was detected, the ECL signal recovered to the same intensity as the target alone. It can be seen that the ECL sensor cannot detect interfering miRNAs other than target miRNAs, and it only has specificity for detecting target miRNA141 and miRNA-155.
In addition, the reproducibility of the ECL sensor was also evaluated. As shown in Fig. 5F, six electrodes from different batches were selected as the test objects. Through detection, their relative standard deviations were $1.8\%$ (miRNA-141) and $2.1\%$ (miRNA-155), respectively, indi­ cating that the constructed ECL sensor has good reproducibility and can be used for subsequent detection of actual samples.
## 3.9. Application of the method in analysis of actual samples
In order to test the application potential of the developed ECL dualwavelength sensor in the detection of actual samples, the standard addition method was first used to the detection of miRNA-141 and miRNA-155 in real human serum. Specific amounts of target miRNA-141 and miRNA-155 were added to healthy human serum obtained from Qingdao People’s Hospital, and 7 gradients of miRNAs serum samples with concentrations ranging from $100\ \mathrm{aM}$ to $100~\mathrm{pM}$ were prepared. After that, the results of three parallel detections for each gradient were used for error analysis, and the recovery rate and relative standard de­ viation under each concentration gradient were obtained. It can be seen from Table S3 that the recoveries of miRNA-141 and miRNA-155 were $95.0\%-105.5\%$ and $95.7\%-103.2\%$ , and the RSDs were $1.8\%{-4.3\%}$ and $1.9\%-5.4\%$ , respectively.
The practical applicability of this biosensor was further verified by detecting miRNAs collected from lysates of human prostate cancer cells (22Rv1), breast cancer cells (MCF-7), and normal breast epithelial cells (MCF-10A). As shown in Fig. S4, with increasing number of 22Rv1, the ECL signal at $535~\mathrm{\nm}$ wavelength corresponding to miRNA-141 increased significantly, while the ECL at $644\ \mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to miRNA-155 remained basically unchanged, which was due to the overexpression of miRNA-141 only in 22Rv1. Correspondingly, with increasing number of MCF-7, which only showed overexpression of miRNA-155, the trend of ECL changes at the two wavelengths was exactly opposite to that of 22Rv1. Unexpectedly, in the detection of MCF-10A with low expression of both miRNAs, the ECL signals at both wavelengths were relatively low and remained unchanged as the amount of MCF-10A increased. It can be seen that the ECL sensor can provide a strong and effective analysis method for the simultaneous detection of multiple miRNAs in actual samples.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, a dual-wavelength ECL biosensor based on a multi­ functional Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs nanocomposites with intra­ molecular self-enhancing effect was constructed for simultaneous detection of multiple miRNAs. Interestingly, the unique ECL lumino­ phore Zr MOFs@PEI@AuAg NCs can display two highly stable ECL at $535~\mathrm{nm}$ and $644~\mathrm{{nm}}$ wavelengths without any non-interfering, which was combined two independent quenching probes to achieve simulta­ neous multi-targets assays without spatial division. In particular, the nanocomposites contain an intramolecular co-reactant PEI to form selfenhancing-ECL structure, generating significantly improved ECL signal due to ultra-close electron exchange in co-reaction process. Further­ more, the combination of highly stable TDN structure and efficient DNA walker cyclic cleavage can not only achieve exponential amplification of trace amounts of target miRNAs, but also avoid cross-hybridization of quenching probes, which can greatly improve the accuracy and sensi­ tivity of ECL detection. Thus the work has developed a new autoenhancing multi-signal ECL material with well performance and pro­ vided a promising ECL strategy for multi-target assay. The present biosensor can become a universal detection platform for various gene by flexible base design, and will have unparalleled application value in clinical analysis and disease detection in the future.
